\journalname{Empirical Software Engineering}
\begin{document}
\title{Do Explanations Transfer? Separating Attribution Information from Transformation Effects in Defect Prediction}
\titlerunning{Separating attribution information from transformation effects}
\author{Priyanshu Kumar \and Kumar Rajnish \and Shubham Kumar}
\authorrunning{Priyanshu Kumar et al.}
\institute{Priyanshu Kumar \and Kumar Rajnish \and Shubham Kumar \at Department of Computer Science \& Engineering, Birla Institute of Technology, Mesra, Ranchi, India \\ \email{priyanshu.kumar@bitmesra.ac.in}}
\date{}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
\textbf{Context:} Feature attributions can be reused to weight, select or augment software metrics, but an improvement over an untreated learner does not isolate the information supplied by an explanation.
\textbf{Objective:} We evaluate whether source-derived Shapley additive explanations improve cross-project defect classification beyond matched numerical and dimensional controls.
\textbf{Method:} A documented external benchmark contains seven project families and 54 static code metrics. Each project is held out in turn. RandomForest and ExtraTrees teachers provide cross-fitted attributions; RandomForest and logistic regression are downstream learners. Three source subsamples and source-project validation support independently tuned treatment/control comparisons and a shared-setting sensitivity. Defect-history variables and all outcome-derived columns are excluded. Earlier experiments are retained as supplementary, exploratory evidence.
\textbf{Results:} Across the fixed external target, teacher and learner grid, mean differences in area under the receiver operating characteristic curve are @@weight@@ for weighting versus uniform scaling, @@selection@@ for attribution-based versus random selection, and @@augmentation@@ for adding attributions beyond prediction augmentation. Target-averaged selection effects range from @@selection_min@@ to @@selection_max@@. All 1,344 external score records are reconstructed from saved predictions.
\textbf{Conclusions:} The results distinguish implementation-specific attribution utility from transformation effects. Teacher and tuning sensitivity, benchmark dependence and retrospective label construction constrain interpretation. The study offers a controlled evaluation protocol rather than a universal transfer claim or a new state-of-the-art predictor.
\keywords{Software defect prediction \and Cross-project prediction \and Feature attribution \and SHAP \and Controlled evaluation \and Reproducibility}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Software defect prediction ranks or classifies software units to support quality assurance. Cross-project prediction is attractive when a target project lacks labelled training data, but its effectiveness depends on the relationship between source and target systems. Comparative evaluations therefore need to separate the effect of the learning algorithm from the data, preprocessing and adaptation choices that surround it \citep{ref13}.

Feature explanations introduce another choice: information extracted from a fitted source model can be reused in a downstream learner. A large attribution might determine which metric is retained, how strongly it is scaled, or which additional explanatory features are appended. These operations are often discussed together as explanation-guided learning, although they change different properties of a predictor. Multiplication changes numerical geometry; selection changes dimensionality; augmentation changes both dimensionality and the information available to a second-stage model.

This distinction creates a problem of experimental identification. If multiplying every input by the same constant produces the same improvement as attribution weighting, the improvement cannot be assigned to the correspondence between feature and attribution. Similarly, adding an out-of-fold prediction may account for an apparent benefit of explanation augmentation. An untreated baseline remains necessary for context, but it is insufficient to isolate attribution information.

We ask a narrower question: \emph{when does source-derived attribution information improve defect classification beyond a matched transformation control?} We compare three operations---weighting, selection and augmentation---with controls that preserve the relevant scale, subset size or prediction channel. We independently tune the treatment and control using only source-project validation, and repeat the comparison with a shared baseline-selected setting. This separates a representation effect under a common setting from performance under equal tuning budgets.

The study follows an audit of an earlier repository. The audit motivated new execution and a subsequent extension to documented data. The external project selection and computational protocol were recorded before generating its model results, but after inspecting the earlier experiments. We describe this as an exploratory extension, not a preregistered confirmation. The main evidence is now a seven-family, leave-one-project-out benchmark with static code metrics and documented release-related labels. Earlier local bug-history data do not determine the primary conclusions.

The contributions are an explicit treatment/control design, a source-group nested evaluation with two attribution teachers, and a reproducible link between predictions and reported effects. The central contribution is empirical identification and its resulting evidence. We do not propose a new learner or claim that a negative aggregate rules out useful explanations under other conditions.

\section{Background and related work}
\subsection{Attributions and downstream utility}
Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) express a fitted model's output through additive feature contributions under an explanation model \citep{ref1}. Here, transfer means reusing source-derived attributions to construct another learner's inputs. It does not mean that the same attribution vector is valid for every target model, nor that an attribution is a causal software property.

Shapley-based feature augmentation studies the use of attribution-derived information in predictive modelling \citep{ref2}. Our prediction-only control isolates the incremental value of attributions beyond a stacking channel. A shared source teacher, explicit project exclusion and source-group tuning make the present implementation an adaptation rather than an exact reproduction. Recent explainability-guided augmentation also targets data-scarce cross-project prediction \citep{ref11}. We do not benchmark every such algorithm; our experiment addresses whether the chosen attribution correspondence contributes beyond its matched controls.

Interpretation can depend on correlated predictors \citep{ref10}. We therefore retain and report attribution rank stability and source redundancy diagnostics. These diagnostics describe the fitted representation; they do not by themselves explain a predictive effect. Recent work on developer-behaviour metrics also distinguishes explanatory rankings from time-aware predictive evaluation \citep{ref12}. Our metric schema and research question differ: we examine transformations of static code metrics, rather than introducing behavioural predictors.

\subsection{Defect data and evaluation design}
Public defect benchmarks enable comparison but inherit the assumptions of their collection processes \citep{ref4,ref5}. In particular, a defect assigned to a release depends on how affected releases and fixes are identified. The external dataset used here provides a documented affected-release label and a corresponding loader \citep{ref9}. We use its realistic binary response, without treating that name as a guarantee of perfect ground truth.

Model selection is part of the evaluated procedure. Optimising settings on evaluation data can introduce selection bias \citep{ref7}. We consequently recompute representations within source-group validation and keep the held-out project outside fitting, tuning and attribution construction. Finally, repeated resampling does not automatically provide independent evidence \citep{ref8}. Our summaries are conditional on the selected benchmark and explicitly account for the distinction between projects and computation repeats.

\section{Research questions and design}
\label{sec:design}
\textbf{RQ1:} On documented external project families, how do source-derived SHAP weighting, selection and augmentation compare with their matched controls under independent tuning?

\textbf{RQ2:} How sensitive are these paired effects to attribution teacher and to independent versus shared-baseline hyperparameter selection?

\textbf{RQ3:} What do attribution stability, source redundancy and target covariate shift reveal about the limits of a single aggregate transfer conclusion?

Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} describes the information boundary. The target project contributes covariates to prediction and responses only to scoring. Unlike the earlier CORAL sensitivity, the external study performs no transductive adaptation or target-labelled training.
@@workflow_figure@@

\section{Data and provenance}
\label{sec:data}
The external data come from the authors' Rnalytica repository, pinned to commit \texttt{1931ca6537b00234b80e3d96f6c76c97524841f2}. URLs and SHA-256 digests are supplied in Online Resource 1. We selected every JIRA family in that inventory that was absent from the earlier benchmark, excluding Camel and Lucene. Within each family we selected the highest listed release whose name did not contain beta or preview. This is a deterministic filename-based selection, not a claim that every selected version was designated production-stable by its project. Table~\ref{tab:data} lists all seven files. No project was removed after observing model performance.
@@dataset_table@@

The source collection describes 54 code metrics and additional process/ownership metrics \citep{ref9}. We use only the explicit 54-column code-metric allowlist, covering complexity, size, coupling and related properties. The response is \texttt{RealBug}, consistent with the authors' loader. Identifiers, heuristic labels, both defect-count columns, and all process/ownership columns are excluded. This avoids using the uncertain local bug-history predictors in the main experiment. The collector's affected-release method provides a documented label construction; we have not independently re-mined or adjudicated every issue report.

Each file has a unique identifier within its release. Metric-vector repetition and shared path strings across projects are audited separately. A shared path string does not establish identical source code, and identical metrics do not necessarily identify the same file. No response-based duplicate removal is performed. Online Resource 1 reports these checks and the precise feature list.

The original local Eclipse and Jureczko/PROMISE experiments remain supplementary. We matched all 997 JDT identifiers and bug labels against the curated package and matched Ant's 21 numeric predictor/response columns row by row. These checks establish agreement with those curated contents, not the original acquisition history or the observation windows of the five local bug-history predictors. Consequently, the uncertain Eclipse results are excluded from the primary evidence. Earlier and external scores are never pooled into a single effect estimate.

This is retrospective, release-based cross-project classification. Sources are not filtered by a common calendar cutoff, and retrospective issue labels need not have been available at a historical deployment date. We therefore make no claim of time-forward operational forecasting. That claim would require label-availability timestamps and a separate chronological evaluation.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Project rotation and preprocessing}
Each of the seven projects serves once as the target, with the remaining six serving as sources. For each seed in \{101,102,103\}, up to 400 rows are sampled without replacement from each source, stratified by its response. Every selected project has more than 400 rows, giving 2,400 training rows per rotation. The cap gives each source equal sample weight and bounds computation; it is not a claim that using all source rows would give the same result. All target rows are evaluated. Source indices and all target predictions are saved.

Median imputation and constant-column removal are fitted on the relevant source training partition. The selected data contain no missing static metrics, but the imputation operation is retained for a defined pipeline. Forest downstream learners receive those metrics directly. Logistic regression receives the signed logarithm $\operatorname{sign}(x)\log(1+|x|)$ followed by a training-fitted RobustScaler. The signed form permits attribution columns to be negative. Learners use balanced class weights; the external protocol uses no synthetic oversampling. This differs explicitly from the conditional SMOTE rule in the earlier study \citep{ref6}.

\subsection{Teachers and cross-fitting}
The attribution teachers are RandomForest and ExtraTrees, each with 32 trees, maximum depth five, minimum leaf size two and balanced class weights. These two related tree families provide a focused teacher sensitivity rather than coverage of all explanation models. Three stratified folds generate one out-of-fold probability and positive-class attribution vector per source row. Imputation for each out-of-fold teacher is fitted on that teacher's training rows. A final teacher trained on all available source training rows predicts and explains the validation or target covariates.

We use tree-path-dependent Tree SHAP. For source out-of-fold attributions $\phi_{ij}$, the nonnegative global relevance is
\begin{equation}
u_j=\max\left(10^{-12},\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\phi_{ij}|\right),\qquad w_j=\frac{u_j}{\sum_{k=1}^{F}u_k}.
\label{eq:weights}
\end{equation}
The floor prevents undefined normalisation; it is not evidence of meaningful relevance. OOF predictions and attributions are used for downstream training, while the final teacher supplies the corresponding held-out representation. This avoids training the second-stage model on in-sample teacher predictions.

\subsection{Treatments and matched controls}
Table~\ref{tab:arms} specifies the complete eight-arm design. Weighting compares $x_jw_j$ with uniform $x_j/F$ and a permutation of the same weight values. Selection retains $\lceil F/2\rceil$ source-ranked features and compares them with a randomly chosen subset of equal size. Augmentation compares original features plus an OOF probability with those same inputs plus the OOF attribution vector. An untreated arm anchors absolute performance, and prediction-only versus untreated isolates the stacking channel.
@@arms_table@@

Each source partition and seed yields one permuted weight vector and one random subset, shared across downstream models. Controls are regenerated inside source-validation folds from those folds' training information. They are not averaged into a single uniform vector before fitting. The limited number of control draws is a stated limitation; seeds index the whole repeated procedure rather than an independent test population.

\subsection{Equal-budget, source-group tuning}
Three GroupKFold splits hold source projects out of validation training, keeping project identities disjoint. In each split, the teacher, OOF representations, relevance, selection and preprocessing are reconstructed using only its training projects. Both candidates for every downstream model and arm are scored on the held-out source projects. RandomForest uses 32 trees and compares depth six with unrestricted depth, retaining minimum leaf size two. Logistic regression compares $C=0.1$ and $C=1$, with liblinear and a 20,000-iteration cap. Convergence warnings are treated as execution failures.

The primary regime chooses each arm's candidate independently by mean source-validation ROC-AUC; ties select the first candidate. The sensitivity regime assigns all arms the candidate selected for the untreated baseline. Thus every primary treatment and control receives the same two-candidate budget and the same source partitions. All final choices are refitted on the complete sampled sources. Neither regime consults the target responses. Equal budgets resolve the earlier shared-setting comparison's tuning asymmetry, although the narrow candidate grid still limits claims about optimally tuned alternatives.

\subsection{Metrics, estimands and verification}
ROC-AUC is primary; average precision and macro-averaged F1 are secondary. Average precision is computed directly rather than as trapezoidal precision--recall area. F1 uses a fixed probability threshold of 0.5. We report absolute scores because a paired improvement alone does not establish useful discrimination or adequate precision on rare defects.

For target $t$, teacher $h$, downstream model $m$, regime $q$ and seed $s$, let $A_{thmqs}^{a}$ be an arm's score. The paired effect and aggregate are
\begin{equation}
\widehat\Delta_{thmq}^{a,b}=\frac{1}{3}\sum_{s=101}^{103}(A_{thmqs}^{a}-A_{thmqs}^{b}),\quad
\overline\Delta_q^{a,b}=\frac{1}{7}\sum_{t=1}^{7}\frac{1}{4}\sum_{h,m}\widehat\Delta_{thmq}^{a,b}.
\label{eq:effect}
\end{equation}
The denominator four reflects two teachers and two downstream models. Repeats reuse target responses, and source training sets overlap across rotations. Consequently, neither the 28 target/teacher/model cells nor the 1,344 score records are independent project replications. We report target ranges and descriptive repeat variation, with no signed-rank tests over seeds, population confidence intervals or equivalence declarations.

The validator reconstructs every metric from saved predictions, verifies the expected result grid, checks source-group exclusion and OOF coverage, and reconstructs hyperparameter choices from stored source-validation scores. A regression test perturbs target covariates and verifies unchanged source relevance and candidate-selection scores. The fitting API accepts no target response argument. These checks reduce specific failure risks; they do not prove universal software correctness.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Absolute performance and matched effects (RQ1)}
The validated external execution comprises 42 target/seed/teacher checkpoints and 1,344 score records. Table~\ref{tab:baseline} reports untreated performance, averaging the three source samples. Untreated predictions are numerically the same across attribution teachers; the table therefore reports one teacher to avoid suggesting additional baseline replication. Average precision should be interpreted alongside the prevalence in Table~\ref{tab:data}.
@@baseline_table@@

Table~\ref{tab:effects} gives the equal-target paired effects for both tuning regimes. Under independent tuning, weighting versus uniform yields @@weight@@ ROC-AUC, selection versus random yields @@selection@@, and adding attributions beyond prediction augmentation yields @@augmentation@@. The selection target means span @@selection_min@@ to @@selection_max@@. These numbers describe different operations; they do not support one binary verdict about all explanation transfer.
@@effects_table@@

Figure~\ref{fig:effects} shows target-level differences, retaining teacher and learner variation in the underlying Online Resource 1 tables. Each point averages the executed teacher/learner cells and the three source samples, not a fitted population effect. The full score grid includes improvements and degradations. A small aggregate must be read with its target range and absolute baseline rather than interpreted as absence of any effect.
@@effects_figure@@

Attribution augmentation has a negative target-averaged ROC-AUC effect on all seven projects under both tuning regimes. Under independent tuning its target means range from @@augmentation_min@@ to @@augmentation_max@@. This consistency is descriptive evidence of degradation for this implementation, not a population significance result. In contrast, the aggregate selection improvement is much smaller than in the earlier fixed-source experiment. Because several design elements and the data changed together, that difference cannot be attributed to any single correction.

The secondary metrics do not yield an identical ordering (Table~\ref{tab:secondary}). Adding attributions beyond predictions changes mean average precision by @@augmentation_ap@@ but mean macro F1 by @@augmentation_f1@@. Selection changes mean average precision by @@selection_ap@@. The threshold-dependent F1 result should not be presented as a contradiction of the ranking metrics or omitted because it differs from them.
@@secondary_table@@

\subsection{Teacher and tuning sensitivity (RQ2)}
Figure~\ref{fig:teacher} separates the RandomForest and ExtraTrees teachers. Across targets and downstream learners, their mean selection effects are @@rf_selection@@ and @@et_selection@@, respectively. Table~\ref{tab:effects} also reports the shared-baseline setting, where selection has mean effect @@shared_selection@@. Differences between the two regimes reflect evaluation of distinct tuning procedures; they are not an unbiased estimate of what a larger search would achieve.
@@teacher_figure@@

@@tuning_interpretation@@

The two teachers are both tree ensembles. Agreement between them would strengthen a conclusion within this tested family, not establish invariance to a linear teacher, a different SHAP dependence assumption or another explanation method. Conversely, disagreement is evidence that the operational definition of source attribution matters and should be reported rather than hidden by pooling.

\subsection{Stability and diagnostic context (RQ3)}
Figure~\ref{fig:stability} reports Spearman rank agreement across source seeds and between teacher-mean relevance vectors. Mean cross-teacher rank agreement is @@teacher_stability@@. These are descriptive correlations of feature rankings; they do not test predictive equivalence. Inactive source features receive zero relevance when vectors are aligned to the common 54-feature schema.
@@stability_figure@@

Source redundancy is the mean absolute pairwise Spearman correlation among active source metrics. Target shift is the mean absolute target-feature median after source-fitted robust scaling. Online Resource 1 reports both per checkpoint and includes the target-level diagnostic plot in Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostics}. They are coarse descriptors and are not exhaustive measures of redundancy or distribution shift.
@@diagnostic_figure@@

We use these quantities to make heterogeneity inspectable, not to select a favourable subgroup or fit a predictive rule from seven targets. A stable attribution ranking can still be unhelpful as a multiplier, while a variable ranking can select similarly informative correlated metrics. Establishing either mechanism would require an intervention or an independently tested hypothesis. No causal explanation of defect generation follows from these diagnostics.

\section{Relationship to the earlier experiments}
The earlier corrected execution contains 14,880 zero-shot score records and 3,560 label-access/preprocessing sensitivity records. It uses two fixed sources per ecosystem, six targets, eight downstream learners and twenty source subsamples. Its additional operationalisations include redundancy-penalised selection and model priors; its CORAL arm uses unlabelled target covariates \citep{ref3}. Those choices differ from the external static-metric protocol, so direct pooling would conflate data, teachers, tuning and learner membership.

For traceability, Online Resource 2 retains the complete earlier manuscript and its result tables. Its weighting-versus-uniform, selection-versus-random and attribution-augmentation effects were +0.0004, +0.0078 and -0.0062 ROC-AUC, respectively. These remain historical exploratory estimates with the stated Eclipse timing limitation; they do not substitute for the external results. Within-target training and exactly ten acquired target labels also answer different information-budget questions from source-only classification.

The earlier preprocessing factorial provides a useful implementation warning. For $c>0$ and a feature with positive interquartile range, robust scaling can cancel multiplication:
\begin{equation}
\frac{cx-\operatorname{median}(cX)}{\operatorname{IQR}(cX)}=
\frac{x-\operatorname{median}(X)}{\operatorname{IQR}(X)}.
\end{equation}
This identity need not hold after an intervening logarithm or when a zero-IQR denominator is replaced by one. The external logistic pipeline applies the logarithm after feature multiplication, so exact cancellation is not assumed. This reasoning motivates matched scaling controls without predicting an inevitable zero effect.

\section{Discussion and implications}
\subsection{What constitutes evidence of attribution utility?}
An attribution-guided representation should be judged against a control that isolates the information it claims to use. For weighting, the multiset of numerical weights and their assignment to features are separable. For selection, the attribution ordering and the removal of dimensions are separable. For augmentation, the explanatory vector and the teacher's prediction channel are separable. The present experiment operationalises these distinctions, making a treatment's incremental effect interpretable within a defined comparison.

The distinction also affects negative evidence. Little difference from uniform weighting does not imply that an explanation is unfaithful to its teacher. It indicates limited downstream benefit from that weighting procedure in the tested setting. A benefit from selection does not establish causality or universal relevance of the selected metrics. These are different constructs, and the discussion should not move from one to another without additional evidence.

The augmentation contrast isolates the incremental addition of the attribution vector beyond the prediction channel, but it also adds dimensions. It cannot distinguish attribution-specific information from a generic dimensionality penalty; an equally sized random-feature augmentation would answer that additional question. We therefore interpret its negative result as a property of the complete implemented augmentation, not proof that SHAP values themselves contain harmful information. For trees, positive monotone scaling ordinarily preserves the ordering of candidate splits, providing a further reason to interpret small weighting effects through the numerical pipeline rather than as a general explanation-quality test.

\subsection{Tuning and practical interpretation}
Reporting both tuning regimes makes clear what is held fixed. Shared settings ask whether the representation helps a particular baseline-selected learner. Independent tuning asks whether it helps after the same limited source-only optimisation budget. Neither is an exhaustive search, but their separation prevents the stronger claim from being inferred from the weaker experiment.

For researchers, the actionable recommendation is to state the information budget, use a matched comparator and report the tuning procedure as part of the treatment. For practitioners, these results support a local evaluation process rather than a blanket prescription to adopt or reject SHAP weighting. Absolute precision, inspection costs and the availability of trustworthy labels matter alongside ranking scores. We have not conducted a developer study or measured defects prevented, so no productivity or deployment benefit is claimed.

\subsection{Scope of the empirical contribution}
The external benchmark extends the project families and rotates the source composition, addressing dependence on the earlier two fixed sources. Restricting primary inputs to documented static metrics removes the uncertain history-feature channel from the main claim. Independent tuning and a second teacher address two specific implementation dependencies. These are concrete improvements in evidential scope, while seven related Java project families, three source subsamples and two tree teachers still leave substantial uncertainty about other settings.

This contribution is a controlled empirical re-evaluation, not an algorithm competition. A future state-of-the-art claim would require reproducing the strongest relevant alternatives under comparable information and tuning budgets. A future forecasting claim would require calendar-aware source selection and labels known at training time. The present results cannot supply those claims by stronger wording.

\section{Threats to validity}
\textbf{Construct validity.} Realistic affected-release labels remain dependent on issue-report accuracy and retrospective linking. The external allowlist removes outcome columns and process histories, but does not independently certify every upstream measurement. Static code metrics are proxies for program properties, not complete descriptions of defects. ROC-AUC, average precision and F1 answer different questions; none measures developer utility directly.

\textbf{Internal validity.} The code records transformations, folds and predictions, and validators check their consistency. Cross-fitting reduces in-sample teacher leakage, and source-group tuning excludes the target. Nevertheless, narrow candidate grids, a 400-row source cap, default settings outside those grids and shared random draws condition the result. The smoke run checked implementation on a reduced configuration; it is not counted as research evidence. No parameter was adjusted in response to full-run target performance.

\textbf{Statistical validity.} Project rotations share training projects and seeds share observations. Teacher/model cells cannot multiply the effective project count. Three repetitions provide limited assessment of source-sample variability, and rare outcomes---including only 26 defective Groovy files---make individual target scores sensitive to particular cases. Target ranges are descriptive, not confidence intervals or equivalence bounds. No post hoc power or population significance claim is made.

\textbf{External validity.} External means new to this repository's previous experiment, not a probability sample of industrial software. Families come from one curated JIRA collection, and some share ecosystems, dependencies and development practices. The filename selection rule excludes beta/preview names but is not a verified release-stability taxonomy. Different languages, newer systems, teachers, explainers, features and chronological deployment settings remain untested.

\textbf{Researcher degrees of freedom.} The extension was designed after earlier results were known. Its selection record and fingerprints document what was fixed before new execution but are not a public preregistration. The new primary/supplementary distinction follows data-quality reasoning and is explicitly disclosed. All completed external arms and both tuning regimes are retained to avoid selectively presenting favourable comparisons.

\section{Conclusion}
We evaluated whether source-derived SHAP information contributes beyond matched transformation controls on seven documented external project families. Under independent source-group tuning, the mean ROC-AUC differences are @@weight@@ for weighting versus uniform scaling, @@selection@@ for attribution selection versus random selection, and @@augmentation@@ for adding attributions beyond prediction augmentation. These estimates are conditional on the specified benchmark and implementation.

The useful distinction is between an explanation's information and the operation used to inject it into a learner. Explicit controls, nested source-only tuning, teacher sensitivity and auditable predictions make that distinction testable. Wider chronological validation and independently tested explanations of heterogeneous effects remain future research, rather than conclusions inferred from the present aggregate.

\section*{Data and code availability}
The primary datasets are available from the Rnalytica repository at the pinned commit stated in Section~\ref{sec:data}. Online Resource 1 contains the download manifest and script, external experiment and validation code, protocols, environment lock, per-instance predictions, score tables and figure generator. It reconstructs the primary study without relying on the local Eclipse data. Online Resource 2 contains the earlier exploratory manuscript and summary artifact, clearly marked as supplementary. A persistent public identifier for the new research artifact has not yet been assigned.

